        "id": 4222956
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-06T06:34:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I intended to make it simple as there is no reason for crosvm to require such appending when it\u0027s already doing \"-cpu host\" behavior. But I\u0027m also fine to make it more failsafe as you suggested, though this path may not be exercised in production.\n\nI did run lots of test cases to catch corner cases including this one. If I simply cut nent by half, the hypercall failed as there is no space to append. But if I keep nent intact but zapping a random entry, it will be filled with the default one as expected.",
      "parentUuid": "80c40953_bff3ec97",
      "revId": "8d980ea6ec4cf59b7f5a2a1ba5195dbc5b0196d9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "901c31cf_558ccac1",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/cpuid.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1528,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-06T12:32:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, I\u0027m probably fine with either keeping it as is for simplicity or making it more failsafe like I suggested.\n\nThe test case in question would be, as I understand: the VMM removes a \"required\" entry, but doesn\u0027t add any \"unsupported\" entries. So pKVM needs to append the \"required\" entry, but there is no room, since there are no \"unsupported\" entries to zap.\n\n...Hmm, what if the required entry that crosvm \"removes\" is a func\u003d4 entry for L4 cache? i.e. if crosvm doesn\u0027t actually remove it, it just calls KVM_GET_SUPPORTED_CPUID on a different pCPU than the one on which pKVM enforces the CPUID later (due to the crosvm\u0027s vcpu thread migrated to a different pCPU by the host Linux scheduler during the time window between these two events), and this func\u003d4 entry doesn\u0027t exist on the initial pCPU but exists on the final one?\n\nSo, we either need to take this scenario into account when addressing the has_func4 special case (which we are discussing in another comment), or we do need a failsafe mechanism like I suggested?\n\nBut even if we address this with has_func4, what about other such potential cases of CPUID leaves that exist on some pCPUs (and whose native values are preserved by __do_cpuid_func() and thus enforced by pKVM) but don\u0027t exist on other pCPUs? Are we sure there will be no other such cases?",
      "parentUuid": "522943d1_10b50753",
      "revId": "8d980ea6ec4cf59b7f5a2a1ba5195dbc5b0196d9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bbfc9d29_ab923516",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/cpuid.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1528,
      "author": {
        "id": 4222956
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-07T00:20:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "for func4, I choose to keep the host provided entries as long as they exist in the new implementation I\u0027m working on. No append even if the native has more cache levels in case of vcpu migration. If crosvm cares about the accuracy, it should try to pin vcpus. It\u0027s not a problem of pkvm.\n\nnative entries are appended to the host buffer only when there is no any func4 entry, as a failsafe mechanism.\n\nthere might be other cases like func4. But as we discussed earlier the entire hybrid support is incomplete. Even w/o enforcement, it\u0027s still a possible case where crosvm get supported cpuid leaves on one cpu but then observed by the guest on a different cpu. somehow vcpu pinning is the only way to ensure correctness in that configuration. and once we have vcpu pinning, then this worry is invalid w/ or w/o enforcement, right?",
      "parentUuid": "901c31cf_558ccac1",
      "revId": "8d980ea6ec4cf59b7f5a2a1ba5195dbc5b0196d9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "36f2a92b_fb17b17a",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/cpuid.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1528,
      "author": {
        "id": 4222956
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-07T04:06:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "security-wise, if a bit related to security (e.g. SMEP) exists on big core but not on small core (hypothetical), and enforcement is done on big core, then pKVM needs to prevent vcpu migration/vmx loading to a small core otherwise guest use of SMEP would be broken. \n\nThen this means pKVM needs to keep track of a list of cpuid bits having a security implication (which we want to avoid as it requires a full study of all CPUID leaves/bits), maintain the difference among big/little cores, record where the enforcement was originally done, and add checks to prevent migration to incompatible cores if such migration may break the earlier enforcement. from security p.o.v. pkvm cannot rely on host to do vcpu pinning.\n\nanother issue is that @vcpu_load in kvm-high is void. there is no way to tell host scheduler when pkvm rejects migration.\n\nlots of complexity, better we can avoid now.\n\nlet me at least check ADL/PTL to see any other cases like func4 and do a study case-by-case.",
      "parentUuid": "bbfc9d29_ab923516",
      "revId": "8d980ea6ec4cf59b7f5a2a1ba5195dbc5b0196d9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
